  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "😈 Demon Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Princes, dukes and marquises of hell: new names, or the 72 spirits of the Ars Goetia."
    },
    "intro": "Demons in the old books had ranks, like nobles at a very bad court: kings, dukes, marquises and presidents, each commanding so many legions. This **demon name generator** gives you new demon names with a rank and a domain (<em>Duke Vasaroth of the Hungry Dark</em>), or the real names of the 72 spirits listed in the <em>Ars Goetia</em>, with the ranks it gives them.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where demon names come from",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The word</strong> comes from the Greek <em>daimōn</em>, which first meant any spirit, good or bad. The evil sense came later.</li><li><strong>Old gods.</strong> Many famous demons began as other people's gods. <em>Baal</em> means \"lord\" and was a Canaanite god; <em>Beelzebub</em> comes from Baal-zebub, \"lord of the flies\"; <em>Astaroth</em> echoes the goddess Astarte.</li><li><strong>The grimoires.</strong> The <em>Ars Goetia</em>, the first part of a 17th-century book of magic called the <em>Lesser Key of Solomon</em>, lists 72 spirits, each with a rank, a number of legions and the things it can teach. Most fictional demon names since have borrowed its sound.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Ranks and legions",
        "content": "<p>The Goetia arranges its spirits like a royal court: <strong>Kings</strong> such as Paimon and Belial, <strong>Dukes</strong> such as Astaroth, <strong>Princes</strong>, <strong>Marquises</strong>, <strong>Earls</strong>, <strong>Presidents</strong> and a lone <strong>Knight</strong>, Furcas. Each entry ends by saying how many legions of spirits it rules, often twenty, thirty or more.</p><p>Choose the \"Goetia style\" format for names written the way the old books write them.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Demons in games",
